/*!
	\page tutorial.html tutorial
	\title Bullet Hell Game Tutorial using Felgo

	\section1 Introduction

	This tutorial describes how to create a simple 2D Bullet Hell Game using Felgo.

	The tutorial will provide a base for the overall game, enabling anyone to further expand on it.
	Here we will go in details on how to implement the player controls and shooting mechanics,
	enemies, their state machines and attack patterns.

	The source code for the finished project is available on
	\l {https://github.com/Anton3311/felgo-challenge} {GitHub}

	\section1 Assets

	The art assets used throughout this tutorial are from Kenney and are available
	\l {https://kenney.nl/assets/tiny-dungeon} {here} under CC0 1.0 License. This asset pack doesn't
	cover all of the needed art, thus some of the missing pieces were hand-drawn in the same style.
	All of the used art assets are available in GitHub Repository under
	\l {https://github.com/Anton3311/felgo-challenge/tree/main/assets/entities} {assets/entities}

	\section1 Project Setup

	Start by creating a new \b{Felgo Games} project in Qt Creator. Sepcifically we will need
	\b{New - Empty Felgo Project} template. The created project will already contain some example
	code in the \c{Main.qml}, which we will reduce down to these 15 lines:

	\qml
	import Felgo 4.0
	import QtQuick 2.0

	GameWindow {
		id: gameWindow
		activeScene: scene
		screenWidth: 960
		screenHeight: 640

		Scene {
			id: scene
		}
	}
	\endqml

	\section1 Player Controls

	As the first major step, we will start with the player controller, to get at least something
	moving on the screen. But before we dive into coding a player controller, we need to prepare
	our scene.

	Firstly, our player is an entity and to be able to work with entities our scene needs an entity
	manager:

	\code
	// This `Item` serves as a container for all entities. Any entities created during the game,
	// will be added to this container.
	Item {
		id: level
	}

	EntityManager {
		id: entityManager
		entityContainer: level
	}
	\endcode

	Now that, we have an entity manager, we can start creating our player.

	The code for the player will live in \c{qml/entities/Player.qml}.

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	EntityBase {
		id: player
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "player"

		Image {
			id: sprite
			// Place the player sprite right in the center of the entity
			anchors.centerIn: parent.anchors.center
			// Disable smoothing to keep the pixel art crisp.
			smooth: false
			source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/player.png")
			width: 32
			height: 32
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And then in our \c{Main.qml} we will first import the code from entities folder:
	\code
	import Felgo 4.0
	import QtQuick 2.0
	import "./entities"
	\endcode

	Finally add our \c{Player} right after the \c{EntityManager}, like this:
	\qml
	Scene {
		id: scene

		EntityManager {
			id: entityManager
			entityContainer: level
		}

		Player {}
	}
	\endqml

	Now lets move onto player controls. The player will rely on physics, that means we first need to
	setup a \c{PhysicsWorld}:

	\qml
	Scene {
		id: scene

		EntityManager {
			id: entityManager
			entityContainer: level
		}

		PhysicsWorld {
			id: physicsWorld
			gravity.y: 0
			updatesPerSecondForPhysics: 60
			velocityIterations: 5
			positionIterations: 5
			debugDrawVisible: false
		}

		Player {}
	}
	\endqml

	Now that we have the \c{PhysicsWorld}, we can add a collider to our player, to turn it into a
	physics object. We will also make our player sprite a child of the collider. The resulting
	player code looks like this:

	\qml
	CircleCollider {
		id: collider
		radius: 16
		// Disable rotations caused by collisions.
		fixedRotation: true

		Image {
			id: sprite
			anchors.centerIn: parent.anchors.center
			smooth: false
			source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/player.png")
			width: 32
			height: 32
		}
	}
	\endqml

	The final step before player is able to move around the scene, is the actual keyboard controls.
	Here we will use a \c{TwoAxisController} with our 4 movement directions mapped to WASD keys.

	\c{TwoAxisController} has two properties we are interested in: \c{xAxis} and \c{yAxis}.

	We can use them to provide a velocity vector for the \c{CircleCollider} like this:

	\code
	linearVelocity: Qt.point(axisController.xAxis, axisController.yAxis)
	\endcode

	\c{TwoAxisController} is only able to notify us about, whenever a button gets pressed or release.
	However, we want to be able to presss and hold a button to move in a desired direction. This
	requires a bit more bookkeeping. We will need to keep track of which of the 4-direction keys are
	currently pressed and update that state on each press/release together with manually updating
	\c{xAxis} and \c{yAxis} properties of the controller.

	Add the \c{bool} properties, corresponding to each of the movement directions, at the start of
	the \c{Player} entity:

	\code
	property bool isMovingLeft
	property bool isMovingRight
	property bool isMovingUp
	property bool isMovingDown
	\endcode

	Our \c{TwoAxisController} we will lok like this:

	\qml
	TwoAxisController {
		id: axisController
		onInputActionPressed: (actionName) => {
			updateActionState(actionName, true);
			updateMovementAxis();
		}
		onInputActionReleased: (actionName) => {
			updateActionState(actionName, false);
			updateMovementAxis();
		}
		inputActionsToKeyCode: {
			"up": Qt.Key_W,
			"down": Qt.Key_S,
			"left": Qt.Key_A,
			"right": Qt.Key_D
		}
	}
	\endqml

	Also don't forget to add the \c{speed} property to the player entity:

	\code
	property int speed: 150
	\endcode

	And finally the helper functions for state tracking and updating controller properties:

	\code
	function updateActionState(actionName, value) {
		if (actionName === "up") {
			isMovingUp = value
		}

		if (actionName === "down") {
			isMovingDown = value
		}

		if (actionName === "left") {
			isMovingLeft = value
		}

		if (actionName === "right") {
			isMovingRight = value
		}
	}

	function updateMovementAxis() {
		let dx = 0;
		let dy = 0;

		if (player.isMovingUp) {
			dy -= 1;
		}

		if (player.isMovingDown) {
			dy += 1;
		}

		if (player.isMovingLeft) {
			dx -= 1;
		}

		if (player.isMovingRight) {
			dx += 1;
		}

		if (dx === 0 && dy === 0) {
			axisController.xAxis = 0;
			axisController.yAxis = 0;
		} else {
			let deltaVectorLength = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
			let deltaScale = 1 / deltaVectorLength * player.speed;

			axisController.xAxis = dx * deltaScale;
			axisController.yAxis = dy * deltaScale;
		}
	}
	\endcode

	One final touch. We can set the \c{mirror} property of the \c{Image} to be computed based
	on the horizontal speed, so the player sprite gets mirrored when the player moves left.

	\code
	mirror: collider.linearVelocity.x < 0
	\endcode

	For the whole player controller to work, we also need to forward all of the key events from the
	scene to our \c{TwoAxisController}. For that we will first need to expose the
	\c{TwoAxisController} inside the player to an outside world, by creating an \c{alias property}:

	\code
	EntityBase {
		id: player
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "player"

		property int speed: 150
		property bool isMovingLeft
		property bool isMovingRight
		property bool isMovingUp
		property bool isMovingDown

		// Expose the controller to an outside world
		property alias controller: axisController
		...
	\endcode

	And forward the events by adding this code in our \c{Main.qml}:

	\code
	Keys.forwardTo: [player.controller]

	Player {
		id: player
	}
	\endcode

	At this point the player should be able to move around the screen.

	\section1 Level

	Now that we have a moving player, we can add a level for the player to move around. Here we will
	go with a rather simple design, the whole level will be a square-shaped arena and it will be its
	own entity. The level background is built out of tiles from the asset pack.

	The code for it is also rather straightforward:

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	Item {
		property int levelSize: 176 * 2
		property int wallThickness: 30
		property int wallCategory: Fixture.Category1

		property alias levelWalls: walls

		// Fill the whole screen with a solid color matching the borders of the background sprite
		Rectangle {
			anchors.fill: parent
			color: "#763b36"
		}

		EntityBase {
			// Display the level background below everything
			id: walls
			width: levelSize
			height: levelSize
			anchors.centerIn: parent

			// Top
			BoxCollider {
				// Move the top one down a bit, so that it covers the top wall
				y: 16
				width: parent.width; height: wallThickness
				bodyType: Body.Static
				categories: wallCategory
			}

			// Down
			BoxCollider {
				y: levelSize - wallThickness
				width: parent.width; height: wallThickness
				bodyType: Body.Static
				categories: wallCategory
			}

			// Left
			BoxCollider {
				width: wallThickness; height: levelSize 
				bodyType: Body.Static
				categories: wallCategory
			}

			// Right
			BoxCollider {
				x: levelSize - wallThickness
				width: wallThickness; height: levelSize
				bodyType: Body.Static
				categories: wallCategory
			}

			Image {
				smooth: false
				width: levelSize
				height: levelSize
				source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/levelBackground.png")
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	The colliders here a placed to match where the walls are in the background sprite. If you set
	\c{debugDrawVisible} to \c{true} in the \c{PhysicsWorld} you will see the next picture:

	\image {levelColliders.png} {Level Colliders}

	Then we add it to our scene and also set \c{z} to some negative value, so that is appears under
	everything in the scene:

	\qml
	LevelBackground {
		id: levelBackground
		z: -100
		anchors.fill: scene
	}
	\endqml

	Now, if you run the game, you will notice, that the player is stuck outside of the level. To fix
	this, we will change the default \c{Player} position to be roughly in the middle of the level:

	\qml
	Player {
		id: player
		x: levelWalls.x + levelBackground.levelSize / 2
		y: levelWalls.y + levelBackground.levelSize / 4 * 3
	}
	\endqml

	And now, we are done with the level part and can move onto other parts of the game.

	\section1 Bullets and Shooting
	
	Now the fun part. Here we will finaly start adding bullets, because a bullet hell game without
	the bullets and bullet patterns isn't a bullet hell.

	In this part, we will design reusable components for us to later be able to implement different
	bullet patterns for the enemies. For example, some of the patterns we will have at the end look like this:

	\image {ghostAttack1.png} {Bullet Pattern Example 1}
	\image {ghostAttack2.png} {Bullet Pattern Example 2}

	First we will start by creating a new entity. A \c{Bullet}:

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	EntityBase {
		id: bullet
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "bullet"

		property int radius: 4
		property point initialImpulse: Qt.point(0, 0)

		CircleCollider {
			id: collider
			radius: bullet.radius
			bodyType: Body.Dynamic
			groupIndex: -1 // make sure that bullets never collide with each other
			linearDamping: 0.1 // make the bullet slows down overtime
			fixedRotation: true
			fixture.onBeginContact: (other, normal) => {
				// On collision with anything - destroy
				bullet.destroy();
			}

			Image {
				smooth: false
				anchors.centerIn: parent
				source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/bullet.png")
				width: 32
				height: 32
			}
		}

		Component.onCompleted: {
			collider.applyLinearImpulse(initialImpulse, collider.body.getWorldCenter())
		}
	}
	\endqml

	\section3 Team Component

	Before we go and implement shooting, we have one more thing to take care of. We want the same 
	\c{Bullet} entity to be reusable and sharable between both the \c{Player} and the enemies.

	That means the bullet also has to act differently, based on which entity created it. For
	example, if the bullet was created by the player, we want it to only collide with the walls and
	the enemies. If the player were to somehow shoot themselfs, the bullet should simply ignore the
	player. The same is for the enemies.

	To solve this, we will introduce our first component. This won't be an \c{EntityBase}, rather a
	simple \c{Item} and it will live in the \c{qml/components/} directory.

	This component will be called \c{Team}.

	The idea here is simple. For example, the player and its bullet will belong to the same team and
	entites on the same team will completely ignore each other (they won't collide, they won't apply
	damage).

	The code for it looks like this (it is just a bunch of properties):

	\qml
	Item {
		// The name of the team, only needed in case of debugging
		property string debugName

		// The category for entities of this team.
		property int entityCategory
		// Categories of entities members of this team are allowed to collide with.
		property int entityCollisionCategories

		// The collision category for bullets.
		property int bulletCategory
		// The mask of collision categories the bullets are allowed to collide with.
		property int bulletCollisionCategories
	}
	\endqml

	Another key factor is that we will only have two instances of this \c{Team} component, placed in
	the scene. And all of the entities will reference them.

	Now we can add both teams to our scene:

	\qml
	Team {
		id: playerTeam
		debugName: "player"
		entityCategory: Fixture.Category2

		// The player can collide:
		// 1. level walls
		// 2. with other players (in case there are many of them)
		// 3. bullets
		entityCollisionCategories: levelBackground.wallCategory
			| Fixture.Category2
			| Fixture.Category4
		bulletCategory: Fixture.Category4

		// Don't include `entityCategory` (`Fixture.Category2`) in the collision mask, so that
		// the player doesn't collide with its own bullets.
		bulletCollisionCategories: levelBackground.wallCategory | Fixture.Category3
	}
	\endqml

	\qml
	Team {
		id: enemyTeam
		debugName: "enemies"
		entityCategory: Fixture.Category3
		// Enemies can collide:
		// 1. level walls
		// 2. with other enemies
		// 3. bullets
		entityCollisionCategories: levelBackground.wallCategory
			| Fixture.Category3
			| Fixture.Category4
		bulletCategory: Fixture.Category4

		// Don't include `Fixture.Category3` which is an `entityCategory`, to make sure that the
		// bullets created by enemies, don't collide with other enemies, as well as, the one
		// that created them
		bulletCollisionCategories: levelBackground.wallCategory | Fixture.Category2
	}
	\endqml

	Now we will need to go and update the player collider, like this:
	\qml
	EntityBase {
		// ...

		property Team team

		// ...

		CircleCollider {
			id: collider

			// ...

			categories: team.entityCategory
			collidesWith: team.entityCollisionCategories

			// ...
		}

		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	And back in our scene, we need to pass the corresponding team instance to the player:

	\qml
	Player {
		id: player
		team: playerTeam
		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	Also don't forget to add \c{import "../components"} in the \c{Player.qml} and
	\c{import "./components"} in the scene file.

	Now if you try running the game, you will see that ... nothing has changed. However, we are
	already a step closer to making the player shoot bullets.

	\section3 Shooting Bullets

	To shoot, the player will have to press and hold the \c{Left Mouse Button}. To handle this, we
	will need to add a \c{MouseArea} to our scene:

	\qml
	MouseArea {
		id: mouseArea
		enabled: true
		// Cover the whole scene
		anchors.fill: scene
		// Make sure mouse hover event is reported, even if non of the mouse buttons were
		// pressed/released.
		hoverEnabled: true
		// Hide the cursor. We will have a custom crosshair sprite instead.
		cursorShape: "BlankCursor"
		onPositionChanged: (event) => {
			// handle mouse movement
		}
		onPressed: (event) => {
			// start shooting
		}
		onReleased: (event) => {
			// stop shooting
		}
	}
	\endqml

	We will also add a crosshair to our scene. For now it will just be a white rectangle. Here we
	bind \c{x} and \c{y} to \c{mouseArea.mouseX} and \c{mouseArea.mouseY}, to make it follow the
	cursor:

	\qml
	Rectangle {
		id: crosshair
		x: mouseArea.mouseX
		y: mouseArea.mouseY
		width: 8
		height: 8
	}
	\endqml

	Now we will go back to \c{Player.qml} here we will need to reference the crosshair by adding a
	new property on the player:

	\qml
	EntityBase {
		id: player

		// ...

		property bool isMovingUp
		property bool isMovingDown
		property Item crosshair

		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	And assing it in the scene:

	\qml
	Player {
		id: player
		// ...
		crosshair: crosshair
		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	One last think to take care of, is that we need to add a reference to the \c{Team} instance in
	the \c{Bullet} entity. From that \c{Team} instance we will use earlier described
	\c{bulletCategory} and \c{bulletCollisionCategories}.

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo
	import "../components"

	EntityBase {
		id: bullet
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "bullet"

		property int radius: 4
		property point initialImpulse: Qt.point(0, 0)
		property Team team

		CircleCollider {
			id: collider
			// ...
			linearDamping: 0.1 // make the bullet slow down overtime
			categories: team.bulletCategory
			collidesWith: team.bulletCollisionCategories
			// ...
		}

		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	And finally we add a \c{shoot} function to the \c{Player} entity. Here we first calculate the
	direction, which is a vector from the player position to the position of the crosshair (which is
	at the mouse cursor). After we've got the direction, we normalize it, scale it by the impulse
	constant and finally use the \c{createEntityFromUrlWithProperties} method of the
	\c{entityManager} to create a bullet entity while passing the initial values for the properties
	we are interested in.

	\b{Note that when creating a new bullet we pass the same \c{team} the player has, to the bullet.}

	\qml
	Player {
		id: player

		// ...

		function shoot() {
			let directionX = crosshair.x - player.x
			let directionY = crosshair.y - player.y

			let directionLength = Math.sqrt(directionX * directionX + directionY * directionY)
			if (directionLength <= 0.0001) {
				// The mouse cursor is almost exactly at the middle of the player, thus there is a chace
				// of a division by zero.
				return;
			}

			directionX /= directionLength;
			directionY /= directionLength;

			let impulse = 150;

			entityManager.createEntityFromUrlWithProperties(
				Qt.resolvedUrl("./Bullet.qml"),
				{
					"x": player.x + sprite.width * 0.5,
					"y": player.y + sprite.height * 0.5,
					"initialImpulse": Qt.point(directionX * impulse, directionY * impulse),
					"team": player.team
				}
			);
		}
	}
	\endqml

	To quickly test how everything works we can temporaly call \c{shoot} whenever we get the
	\c{pressed} signal in the \c{mouseArea}:

	\qml
	MouseArea {
		id: mouseArea
		enabled: true
		// ...
		onPressed: (event) => {
			player.shoot();
		}
		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	This is not exactly what we initially intended, but we can quickly see how everything works.
	Right now \c{shoot} will only be called when the butten gets pressed, so you will need to press
	it multiple times to shoot many bullets.

	Now that we've verify that it works, we can finish this features. The goal was to able to press
	and hold to shoot bullets continuosly. For that we will add a \c{Timer} to our player. The timer 
	will periodically call the \c{shoot} function. And we will control it (enable or disable)
	whenever we get \c{pressed} or \c{released} signals from the \c{mouseArea}.

	\qml
	Player {
		// ...
		property int shootInterval: 200
		// ...
		Timer {
			id: shootTimer
			repeat: true
			running: false
			interval: shootInterval
			onTriggered: {
				shoot();
			}
		}

		function startShooting() {
			shootTimer.restart();
		}

		function stopShooting() {
			shootTimer.running = false;
		}

		function shoot() {
			// ...
		}
	}
	\endqml

	Back in the \c{mouseArea} signal handlers we will call the new \c{startShooting} and
	\c{stopShooting} functions:

	\qml
	MouseArea {
		// ...
		onPressed: (event) => {
			player.startShooting();
		}
		onReleased: (event) => {
			player.stopShooting();
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And that is pretty much it, for this mechanic.

	\section1 Damagable Component

	Before we dive into implementing enemies we need to prepare some more common building blocks.
	Besides the \c{Team} being common between both the player and the enemies, one more thing they 
	share is health. So here we will add exactly that. Health in our game will be
	reperesanted/handled by the \c{Damagable} component.

	This a rather simple component and doesn't require much code. It will have a single function
	for appling damage, and a couple of signals to notify the outside world:

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	// This component implements health tracking + damage handling
	//
	// Can be shared by both the player and the enemies
	Item {
		property Team team
		property int health
		property int maxHealth

		Component.onCompleted: {
			health = maxHealth
		}

		signal damageApplied(damage: int)
		signal death

		function applyDamage(damage) {
			if (health == 0) {
				// Already dead => early return here, to avoid emitting `death` signal one more time.
				return;
			}

			// Clamp the damage, so that the health doesn't become negative
			//
			// Also when reporting damage to the event system, we want to
			// send the actual damage amount that was applied
			if (damage > health) {
				damage = health
			}

			if (damage > 0) {
				health -= damage;
				damageApplied(damage);
			}

			if (health == 0) {
				death()
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	We can add this component to our player, while also exposing it through the \c{damagable}
	property:

	\qml
	Player {
		// ...
		property alias damagable: myDamagable

		// ...

		Component.onCompleted: {
			damagable.team = team
		}

		Damagable {
			id: myDamagable
			maxHealth: 6
		}

		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	Now the player has health, however we can't see how much of it is currently left, so we will add
	a little UI for that. The UI will be rather simple and just be a row of either full or empty
	hearts. Two \c{Repeater}s will do the trick.

	The first repeater will place \c{damagable.health} number of full hearts and the other one will
	place \c{damagable.maxHealth - damagable.health} number of empty hearts.

	We start by creating a new file under \c{qml/ui}

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	import "../components"

	Item {
		id: self
		property Damagable damagable
		property url fullHeartImage: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/heartFull.png")
		property url emptyHeartImage: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/heartEmpty.png")

		x: 0
		y: 0
		height: 16
		width: parent.width

		Row {
			width: parent.width
			height: parent.height
			Repeater {
				model: damagable.health
				Image {
					source: self.fullHeartImage
					fillMode: Image.PreserveAspectCrop
					width: 32
					height: 32
					smooth: false
				}
			}
			Repeater {
				model: damagable.maxHealth - damagable.health
				Image {
					source: self.emptyHeartImage
					fillMode: Image.PreserveAspectCrop
					width: 32
					height: 32
					smooth: false
				}
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And finally add it to the scene:

	\qml
	import "./ui"

	Scene {
		// ...

		Player {
			// ...
		}

		MouseArea {
			// ...
		}

		PlayerHealthBar {
			damagable: player.damagable
		}
	}
	\endqml

	After the above changes we reach the next result:

	\image {playerHealthBar.png} {Player Health Bar}

	\section1 BulletEmitter Component

	One last thing we have to tackle here, is extracting creation of bullets into its own component.

	This component will be called a \c{BulletEmitter} and will provide some common logic and make it
	easier for us to implement different attack patterns.

	The process for adding a new component is already well-known. Here we pretty much want to
	extract the code we already have in the player entity, into a new place, with some minor
	adjustments: the bullets will be emitted relative to some "origin" entity. The users of this
	component will need to provide the initial position of the bullet relative to that origin. Most
	likely the origin will be the player or the enemy itself.

	Here we have a code snippet extracted from the \c{Player.qml}:

	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo

	Item {
		id: self
		property Team team
		property url bulletPrefab
		property Item emissionOrigin
		property point emissionOriginOffset: Qt.point(0, 0)

		// Here `positionX` and `positionY` are local to the `emissionOrigin` entity
		function emit(positionX, positionY, directionX, directionY, impulse) {
			let forceVector = Qt.point(directionX * impulse, directionY * impulse)

			entityManager.createEntityFromUrlWithProperties(
				bulletPrefab,
				{
					"x": positionX + self.emissionOrigin.x + self.emissionOriginOffset.x,
					"y": positionY + self.emissionOrigin.y + self.emissionOriginOffset.y,
					"initialImpulse": forceVector,
					"team": self.team
				}
			);
		}
	}
	\endqml

	In \c{Player.qml} we can now replace everything related with the \c{BulletEmitter}:

	\qml
	Player {
		// ...

		Component.onCompleted: {
			damagable.team = team

			// The `Bullet`s require a `Team` instance. `BulletEmitter` passes one to every created
			// bullet.
			bulletEmitter.team = team
			// Set the center of the player as the bullet emission origin.
			bulletEmitter.emissionOrigin = player
			bulletEmitter.emissionOriginOffset = Qt.point(sprite.width * 0.5, sprite.height * 0.5)
		}

		// ...

		TwoAxisController {
			// ...
		}

		BulletEmitter {
			id: bulletEmitter
			bulletPrefab: Qt.resolvedUrl("Bullet.qml")
		}

		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	And in the \c{shoot} function of the \c{Player} we replace
	\c{entityManager.createEntityFromUrlWithProperties} with \c{bulletEmitter.emit}:

	\qml
	Player {
		// ...
		function shoot() {
			// ...

			let impulse = 150;

			// Since the origin entity and the offset in `BulletEmitter` are set to the center of
			// the player, the initial position of the bullet is (0, 0).
			bulletEmitter.emit(0, 0, directionX, directionY, impulse);
		}
	}
	\endqml

	\section1 Enemies

	Now we've reached the last major part of this tutorial and the game: the enemies. In the next
	sections we won't go into as much of details as in the previos ones, since we already have the
	majority of building blocks in place, and right now we will mostly need to assemble them.

	As a part of this tutorial we will create a single enemy, which will be a ghost.

	\div {class="pixel-art-tile"}
		\image {ghost.png} {Ghost}
	\enddiv

	\section3 Enemy Entity

	We start of as usual by creating a new entity file. The majority of it can be inherited from the
	\c{Player}, specifically: \c{Damagable}, \c{CircleCollider} and \c{BulletEmitter} (which will
	come in handy later).

	After taking parts of the player and appling some minor modifications like changing the
	\c{maxHealth} and the \c{sprite}, and handling \c{death} signal of the \c{Damagable}, we get
	something like this:
	
	\qml
	import QtQuick
	import Felgo
	import "../components"

	EntityBase {
		id: self
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "ghost"

		property alias damagable: myDamagable
		property Team team

		Component.onCompleted: {
			bulletEmitter.team = team
			damagable.team = team
		}

		Damagable {
			id: myDamagable
			maxHealth: 4
			onDeath: {
				self.destroy();
			}
		}

		BulletEmitter {
			id: bulletEmitter
			emissionOrigin: self
			bulletPrefab: Qt.resolvedUrl("./Bullet.qml")
			emissionOriginOffset: Qt.point(sprite.width * 0.5, sprite.height * 0.5)
		}

		CircleCollider {
			id: collider
			categories: team.entityCategory
			collidesWith: team.entityCollisionCategories
			radius: 16
			fixedRotation: true
			linearDamping: 1

			Image {
				id: sprite
				anchors.centerIn: parent.anchors.center
				smooth: false
				source: Qt.resolvedUrl("../../assets/entities/ghost.png")
				width: 32
				height: 32
				mirror: collider.linearVelocity.x < 0
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And we can quickly add it to the scene:

	\qml
	Ghost {
		x: levelBackground.levelWalls.x + levelBackground.levelSize / 2
		y: levelBackground.levelWalls.y + levelBackground.levelSize / 4
		team: enemyTeam
	}
	\endqml

	Now if you were to launch the game and test the enemy, you will notice that the enemy doesn't
	seem to recieve any damage and more over seems to be immortal. The reason for that is rather
	simple: we forgot to implement one tiny but crucial feature of the \c{Bullet}. Right now when a
	bullet hits an entity it doesn't apply any damage. It simply destroys itself.

	Let's fix that. The fix is pretty straightforward. The one thing we need to keep in mind,
	is that if the entity and the bullet are on the same \c{Team} we don't apply the damage.

	\qml
	EntityBase {
		id: bullet
		entityId: "entity"
		entityType: "bullet"

		// ...

		CircleCollider {
			// ...
			fixture.onBeginContact: (other, normal) => {
				let target = other.getBody().target;
				let damagable = target.damagable;

				// The bullet might have hit some other entity like a wall, that doesn't have a
				// `Damagable` component.
				if (damagable !== undefined) {
					if (damagable.team === bullet.team) {
						// The entity is one the same => don't apply any damage and don't destroy
						// itself.
						return;
					}

					// To keep it simple apply a hardcoded damage amount. You might, however, want
					// to make it configurable, if you want to extend the game even further.
					damagable.applyDamage(1);
				}

				bullet.destroy();
			}
		}
		
		// ...
	}
	\endqml

	Now we can launch the game and verify that the enemy is indeed no longer immortal.

	\section3 Making Enemies shoot

	After creating a base for our enemy, we can start implement its attacks. For now we will keep it
	simple, and make it periodically shoot bullets. This is where we will need the
	\c{BulletEmitter}. 

	This doesn't require much. We just need a single timer, that will periodically fire (every 1.2
	seconds) and trigger the attack pattern.

	There is also one problem we quickly need to take care of. We don't know where the player is the
	world. To be able to shoot bullets in the direction of the player, we need to know the exact
	position.

	At this point, there are many options to handle this. For example, we could use colliders to
	check whenever a potential target is in range. However, we will go with a way simpler solution.
	In our case the only entity on the player's team is the player, there are no other players. That
	means, all the possible options for which entity could be a target, boil down to just the
	player. That said, we can simply pass the player instance from scene down to each enemy.

	That is what the \c{target} property in the \c{Ghost.qml} for.

	\qml
	EntityBase {
		// ...

		property EntityBase target

		// ...

		Timer {
			id: attackTimer
			interval: 1.2 * 1000
			repeat: true
			running: true
			onTriggered: {
				// attack pattern goes here
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	Back in the scene:

	\qml
	Ghost {
		// ...
		target: player
	}
	\endqml

	Now we can dive into implementing the actual attack pattern. We can start with a rather simple
	one, which involes shooting some number of bullets in all directions. Let's say that number is
	12 bullets and they will be evenly spaced across the circumference of the circle.

	The math for this dead simple:

	\qml
	Timer {
		id: attackTimer
		// ...
		onTriggered: {
			let count = 12;
			let angleStep = 2 * Math.PI / count
			for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {
				let angle = angleStep * i;

				let x = Math.cos(angle);
				let y = Math.sin(angle);

				bulletEmitter.emit(0, 0, x, y, 150);
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	The final result looks like this (note, that on this screenshot, some of the bullets have hit
	the walls and disappeared):

	\image {ghostAttack1.png} {Ghost Attack}

	This was pretty neat, however I want give an example of a more sofisticated attack pattern. This
	attack pattern involves shooting bullets directly towards the player, while applying some
	variation in direction and creating bullets one by one with some time in between.

	This pattern is already at the limits of what the current \c{BulletEmitter} allows. It allows us
	to immediately create bullets. However, we want quite the opposite. We could add another timer
	to the \c{Ghost} enemy, that delays the creation of bullets, but it would complicate things.
	Furthermore we want to keep it reusable so that other pattern implementation could benefit from
	it.

	The idea here is to introduce a queue and a \c{Timer} to the \c{BulletEmitter}. The timer will
	fire periodically and pop an entry from the front of the queue and spawn a bullet. And in the 
	enemy attack implementation we will simply push bullet entries to the end of the queue.

	The modifications to the \c{BulletEmitter} look like this:

	\qml
	Item {
		// ...
		property point emissionOriginOffset: Qt.point(0, 0)

		property var queue: []

		Timer {
			running: true
			interval: 100
			repeat: true
			onTriggered: {
				if (queue.length === 0) {
					return;	
				}

				let first = self.queue.shift();
				emit(first.x,
					first.y,
					first.directionX,
					first.directionY,
					first.impulse);
			}
		}

		function enqueueForEmission(positionX, positionY, directionX, directionY, impulse) {
			self.queue.push({
				"x": positionX,
				"y": positionY,
				"directionX": directionX,
				"directionY": directionY,
				"impulse": impulse
			})
		}

		// Here `positionX` and `positionY` are local to the `emissionOrigin` entity
		function emit(positionX, positionY, directionX, directionY, impulse) {
			// ...
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And now we are ready to implement our new sofisticated attack pattern. The code for this pattern
	is actually quite similar to the first one we had. What changed is how we compute the angle. 
	Before we just used to evenly space things out across the circumference. However, right now we
	use \c{atan2} to compute the angle between the X axis and the direction towards the player. Then,
	when spawning bullets, we apply a random jitter to that angle, and use our newly created
	\c{enqueueForEmission}.

	\qml
	EnemyBase {
		// ...

		function randomInRange(min, max) {
			let value = Math.random();
			return min + value * (max - min)
		}

		Timer {
			// ...
			onTriggered: {
				let dx = target.x - self.x
				let dy = target.y - self.y
				let angleTowardsTarget = Math.atan2(dy, dx)

				let spread = Math.PI / 6;
				let count = 4;
				for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {
					let spreadAngle = randomInRange(-spread / 2, spread / 2)
					let angle = angleTowardsTarget + spreadAngle

					let x = Math.cos(angle);
					let y = Math.sin(angle);
					bulletEmitter.enqueueForEmission(0, 0, x, y, 150);
				}
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	Our new attack pattern is done. Although, we had to get rid of the first one, we can still use
	in a different place. For example, whenever the enemy dies. This shouldn't be that difficult to
	implement. We only have to move the pattern code to the \c{death} signal handler, where we
	already have \c{self.destroy()}.

	\qml
	Damagable {
		id: myDamagable
		maxHealth: 4
		onDeath: {
			let count = 12;
			let angleStep = 2 * Math.PI / count
				for (let i = 0; i < count; i += 1) {
					let angle = angleStep * i;

					let x = Math.cos(angle);
					let y = Math.sin(angle);

					bulletEmitter.emit(0, 0, x, y, 150);
				}

			self.destroy();
		}
	}
	\endqml

	That is pretty much it. We can now test our enemy.

	\image {ghostAttack2.png} {Ghost Attack 2}

	\section3 Enemy State Machine

	And finally, the last thing we will tackle in this tutorial is a simple enemy AI, since right
	now the enemy, besides the attacks, doesn't do much. The AI will be a state machine with only 2
	states: attacking and following. In terms of the attack state, there isn't much, we've already
	implemented and will only need to port it to work with the state machine. That means, we are
	left with implementing the state mchine and the \c{following} state.

	The \c{following} state isn't complicated either, it simply involves moving the enemy towards 
	the player, until it is in range. When in range we switch the enemy into the \c{attacking}
	state.

	To implement our state machine, we will start with the next modifications to our enemy code:

	\qml
	EntityBase {
		// ...
		state: "follow"
		states: [
			State {
				name: "follow"
				PropertyChanges { target: attackTimer; running: false }
			},
			State {
				name: "attack"
				PropertyChanges { target: attackTimer; running: true }
			}
		]
	}
	\endqml

	We will also need to run some code everyframe. For example, every frame we will check whether
	the player is still in range and also try to move the enemy towards the player.

	That will be taken care of by a \c{Timer} that runs every \c{16ms} (assuming we are running at
	60 frames per second).

	\qml
	Timer {
		id: updateTimer 
		interval: 16
		running: true
		repeat: true
		onTriggered: {
			// Here we update the whole state machine of this enemy

			switch (state) {
			case "attack":
				// Actual bullet emission is ran on its own timer
				//
				// So here we only have to check, whether the target is still in range
				if (!isTargetWithinRange()) {
					// No target in range, go back to following
					state = "follow"
				}

				break;
			case "follow":
				if (isTargetWithinRange()) {
					// The target is in range, the enemy can start attacking.
					state = "attack";
				} else {
					// The target is not in range.
					//
					// Move towards it.
					let dx = target.x - self.x
					let dy = target.y - self.y
					let distanceToTarget = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy)

					// Normalize the direction
					dx /= distanceToTarget;
					dy /= distanceToTarget;

					let speed = 80
					collider.linearVelocity = Qt.point(dx * speed, dy * speed);
				}

				break;
			}
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And also add the helper function \c{isTargetWithinRange}:

	\qml
	EntityBase {
		// ...
		property int attackRange: 220

		// ...

		// This is this a simple distance check.
		function isTargetWithinRange() {
			let dx = target.x - self.x
			let dy = target.y - self.y
			let distanceToTarget = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy)
			return distanceToTarget <= attackRange;
		}
	}
	\endqml

	And these were the final changes, we need to make to get the state machine working. Now we have 
	a fully working enemy with two state and two distinct attack patterns.

	\section1 Conclusion

	After following this tutorial you will end up with a pretty solid base for a game in a Bullet
	Hell genre.

	Here we went through implementing player controls, bullet mechanics and shooting, an enemy, its
	state mchine and attack patterns. Besides that, this tutorial describes how were all the game
	components were created in way that allows, you to assemble them without much problems to create
	new entities, enemies and their attack patterns.

	This game, however, still lacks some features, like:
	\list
	\li a main menu
	\li game over and restarting logic
	\li spawning of waves
	\li sound effects
	\endlist
*/
